{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Yosef died, etc., and the children of Israel, etc.</b> One must examine for what purpose it returned a second time to inform us of the death of Yosef, when He has already made us hear (Genesis 50:26) ״וַיָּמָת יוֹסֵף בֶּן״, etc.? Further, one must know the intent of the verse's informing us that the brothers and the entire generation died. Further, what connection is there between the death of the brothers and the generation and His saying ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״? Now, the Maggid of the Maharik told him the reason for this juxtaposition, and it is by way of sod. Further, one must know the reason for His saying it in this order — ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, etc. — for if He came to inform us of the fruitfulness and proliferation of the children of Israel, He should have said in this order: ״וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּשְׁרְצוּ וְגוֹ׳ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״." "<b>Indeed</b>, two verses that come as one to inform us of the chain of causation of the beginning of the enslavement and its causes — and they are four in number. The first — the death of Yosef: for had Yosef been alive, he would have ensured that the Egyptians would not rule over them. From this you learn that all the while Yosef was alive, Israel was in the land of Egypt quiet and pampered." "The second — the death of the brothers: for all the while one of the brothers was alive, the Egyptians would honor them, as they of blessed memory explained (Sotah 13a) regarding the verse (Genesis 50:14) ״וַיָּשָׁב יוֹסֵף וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶחָיו״, etc., after he buried his father — see their words there." "The third — the death of the entire generation. Meaning, all seventy souls, for all of them were esteemed in their [the Egyptians'] eyes, and through their mediation there was no opening to enslave them. Or it may be because they were men of understanding, and there is no possibility for the Egyptians to outsmart them with wisdom that begins with soft speech, for the men of that generation were wise and would recognize the stratagem and would not accept having anything done to them in any manner." "The fourth — ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, etc.: for had there not been the great surge that ״they were fruitful and swarmed, etc., exceedingly, etc., and the land was filled,״ etc. — they would not have set their hearts to seek devices; rather, the great surge is what surrounds the mediation of the removal of the preventive — that is, Yosef and the brothers and the entire generation. And with this the saying of <b>וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> is reconciled, and He did not say ״וַיִּפְרוּ״, etc., for He did not come to inform us of the matter of proliferation but rather to inform us that this is what brings about the exile." "Further, <b>וַיָּקָם מֶלֶךְ חָדָשׁ</b> ('and a new king arose') — and without this one thing among all the matters arranged, there would have been no enslavement. And according to the one who says (Sotah 11a) that 'new' means that his decrees were renewed, the preceding causes brought him to renew his thinking differently from before. And understand this." "Now, according to the plain sense of things, it indicates that as long as one of that generation was alive, he would have prevented the enslavement. And it is difficult: if so, the verse should have said only 'and all that generation died' — for even during the lifetime of any one of that generation there would have been no enslavement, and there would have been no need to say 'not the death of Joseph' and 'not the death of the brothers,' for by a fortiori reasoning there would have been no enslavement in their days. And even according to their words, their memory for blessing (Seder Olam Rabbah 3), who said that as long as one of the tribes was alive there was no enslavement — it is difficult: why was it necessary to mention the death of Joseph? It is a fortiori from the brothers." "Or perhaps the verse intends to say that there were gradations in the matter: for with the death of Joseph they descended from their greatness — they had been superior to the Egyptians — and became equal to them; and with the death of the brothers they descended further from their level, for in the Egyptians' eyes they became despised, but they were not yet enslaved by them; and with the death of the entire generation, [the Egyptians] began to enslave them, in the midst of their jealousy when they saw the extraordinary proliferation of Israel, so that the land of Egypt was filled with them." "<b>And if</b> you will say: but our Rabbis, their memory for blessing, said only 'as long as one of the tribes' etc., whereas according to our words it is even from the seventy [souls]. Know that this matter was not decisively resolved by our Rabbis, their memory for blessing, and this is their language in Seder Olam (Seder Olam Rabbah 3): 'From the death of Levi until Israel went out of Egypt were 116 years; the enslavement was no more and no less than 86 — the years of Miriam, who was named for the bitterness' — end quote. See that there is no decisive statement that from the death of Levi — who was the last of the tribes — the enslavement began. And the plain sense of the verse, as I see it, is that they did not say 'come, let us deal shrewdly with him' except after the death of all that generation, and the verse equated [Joseph] to all his brothers."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:22.380109860Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}